<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayushiridara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1338–<a href="/years/1378">1378</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Biligtü Khan · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ayushiridara, known as Biligtü Khan, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghon-temur">Toghon Temür</a>, the last Yuan emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> from 1370 to 1378. Driven from China by the Ming, he re-established the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> and still claimed to rule all China, and his general Köke Temür defeated a large Ming invasion of Mongolia in 1372.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ayushiridara.jpg" alt="Ayushiridara" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avishay · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BILEGT_KHAAN_AYUSHRIDAR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ayushiridara was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghon-temur">Toghon Temür</a> and Lady Gi, who came from a lower-ranked aristocratic family of Goryeo and had been a palace maid. Many Mongol noyans preferred another heir, and only after the purge of the family of Empress Danashiri and the death of Bayan were mother and son accepted at court. He began learning Chinese at ten in the house of the minister Toqto'a. In 1354 Ayushiridara, Lady Gi and the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> accused Toqto'a of corruption while he was fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a>, and Toqto'a was exiled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As crown prince from 1353 he drew the court into faction. With his mother he forced the first minister Tai ping to resign, and when the opposition leader Bolad-Temür occupied the capital in 1364 he fled to the general Köke Temür. After Bolad-Temür was murdered on the emperor's secret order, Ayushiridara pressed for his father's abdication, but the emperor only made him his lieutenant. In 1368 the Ming overthrew the Yuan and the court fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghon-temur">Toghon Temür</a> died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingchang">Yingchang</a> in 1370, the Ming took the city, capturing Ayushiridara's son Maidarbal, and Ayushiridara escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Enthroned as Biligtü, the Intelligent, he took the era name Xuanguang and made Köke Temür his commander in chief. In 1372 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> sent 150,000 men into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. The central division under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-da">Xu Da</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuul-river">Tuul River</a> and was routed, the eastern division was beaten on the Kherlen and the Orkhon and near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, and the western one withdrew. Biligtü Khan asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongmin-of-goryeo">King Gongmin</a> of Goryeo for help, calling him a fellow descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, but Gongmin refused. In 1375 the Mongol official Naghachu invaded the Liaodong Peninsula, and Köke Temür died that year. Biligtü Khan died in 1378 and his half-brother Tögüs Temür succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Biligtü Khan Ayushiridara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Ayushiridara</b> re-established the court at Karakorum, still claiming rule over all China, and his generals defeated a Ming invasion of Mongolia in 1372.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.2,102.82&amp;year=1364" aria-label="Karakorum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ayushiridara"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5739 6014h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5739 6014h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.2,102.82&amp;year=1364">What was here in 1364? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
